  {"id":"19636605-66ad-4f37-8040-6290cd403cf5","term":"Buffer pedal","verdict":"NEEDS_REVISION","suggestions":[{"field":"plain_english","severity":"High","issue":"plain_english says the buffer 'boosts a guitar's weak signal,' which is technically wrong and contradicts the entry's own definition ('unity-gain buffer... copies the signal's voltage without changing its level') and its own common_mistakes ('Thinking a buffer boosts volume, when it is unity gain and changes impedance, not level'). A buffer does NOT boost signal level/volume. Web-verified via effectrode.com (How to Use a Guitar Buffer Pedal): 'a buffer is a unity gain (0dB) amplifier circuit with a high input impedance and low output impedance'; it preserves level and only lowers output impedance. This internal self-contradiction plants the exact misconception the entry elsewhere warns against.","suggestion":"Rewrite the plain_english opener to avoid the 'boost' framing, e.g.: 'A small box that strengthens a guitar's signal without making it louder. By lowering the signal's impedance, it lets the sound run through long cables and many pedals without losing its bright, sparkly highs.'"}]},
